"""Deeper figure-specific reasoning and evidence-based takeaways for review set 1-32.

Numbers are the stable 32-figure review IDs. Selection/renumbering is deferred.
"""
DETAILS = {
1: (
"The key comparison is not which panel contains more groups: every panel contains exactly the same 590 edges. A neighborhood that looks separated in spring layout may be less visually isolated under spectral placement. Checking the actual links distinguishes graph structure from layout geometry. Slovenia's degree of 12 exceeds the mean 6.59, but this is a property of a symmetrized five-nearest-neighbor construction, not evidence of national influence or physical network capacity. This figure earns its place as the required controlled layout experiment.",
"The same 179-country, 590-edge graph creates different visual neighborhoods under different layouts. Verify an apparent group using its links; geometric separation alone is not a discovered community."),
2: (
"This view has a different evidential role from a data-result plot: it documents the browser interface and available inspection controls. In its current unselected state it largely repeats the network overview, so it does not justify a new country-level conclusion. Its value increases only when a reader actually selects a country, inspects neighbors and compares the retained edges. Keeping the interactive application is essential even if this screenshot is later omitted from the main report; implementation completeness and screenshot count are different issues.",
"The explorer enables country-level inspection, but this unselected screenshot adds little beyond the layout comparison. Keep the working interaction even if the duplicate overview image is removed."),
3: (
"Read one polyline across all four measured axes before comparing the whole collection. Low landline intercepts combined with much higher mobile intercepts illustrate why one infrastructure indicator cannot summarize a country. However, a steep segment between differently scaled axes is not a numerical growth rate, and crossings do not show temporal transitions. This is a single-year profile view. The straight-line baseline is retained because it lets the reader check whether later bundling changes readability while preserving the measured values.",
"A country's mobile subscription intensity, fixed infrastructure and adoption need not move together in a 2020 profile. Four explicit axes preserve those differences; subscriptions above 100 remain counts of subscriptions, not people."),
4: (
"The intervention here is the 50% region-centroid bundling, not a change in the dataset. Compare the same axis intercepts with the straight baseline: their locations should agree even where the connecting paths bend noticeably. A visually tight regional bundle may result partly from the rendering rule itself. It therefore helps a reader follow known group membership but is not independent evidence that regions form compact statistical clusters. Precision still comes from the intercepts, hover values and brushing, rather than from the curves between axes.",
"Moderate bundling makes supplied regional paths easier to follow without moving their measured axis values. The resulting bundles visualize the grouping rule; they do not discover or prove regional clusters."),
5: (
"China and India together account for more than half of the 2.574 billion included internet users in the Asia view. This concentration explains the large rectangles without requiring either country to have the highest adoption percentage. The color channel answers that separate question. A large population can produce many users even at incomplete adoption, whereas a small country with near-universal adoption can occupy little area. The denominator is the included-country total, so the result should not be generalized to missing countries or treated as a population-weighted average adoption rate.",
"China and India dominate the included Asian user count, but rectangle size does not establish universal adoption. Read area as scale of use and color as prevalence; the two answer different questions."),
6: (
"This zoom contains 24 high-adoption Asian countries, compared with the 43-country regional view, and a much smaller included user total. The hierarchy first asks which countries belong to the 75-100% band and then where they are located. It does not report users lost after changing the hierarchy. The displayed leaves differ between these two zoom states, so they should not be compared as equal-population experiments. A controlled comparison of the hierarchy algorithms would show the same full leaf universe at the root before zooming.",
"The 497.5 million users in this view belong to a selected high-adoption subset, not a reduced version of Asia's population. Remodeling changes the route of inquiry; the different zoom states also change which countries are displayed."),
7: (
"The country rows are a deliberately selected set of low/high 2020 examples within regions. Their contrast is useful for showing heterogeneity that a regional median can hide, but it is not an estimate of how common each trajectory is. Follow one row horizontally: changing saturation indicates distance from the 50% baseline, while the sign color indicates its side. A row may become less dark red as adoption rises without becoming blue. The construction example later explains that apparent paradox using the original values.",
"Selected countries illustrate contrasting routes toward majority internet use. The selection demonstrates variation rather than its prevalence, and a red horizon can still represent improving adoption below 50%."),
8: (
"Europe's 2020 included-country median of 86.86% exceeds Africa's 31.89% by about 55 percentage points. Reading that endpoint together with the trajectories distinguishes the timing of crossing the 50% milestone from the final level reached. The fixed 159-country panel prevents changing availability from explaining the regional time paths; six classified regions contain 158 of those countries. Equal-country medians still hide within-region variation, so the country horizons and regional ridgelines are necessary companions rather than interchangeable repetitions.",
"On a fixed country panel, regional progress is uneven: Europe crosses majority use earlier, while Africa's median is still 31.89% in 2020. Regional improvement does not establish that the gap has disappeared."),
9: (
"The median shifts from 2.20% to 71.37%, a rise of 69.17 percentage points, while the distribution remains spread across adoption levels. These are two different findings: a typical included country has progressed substantially, yet the entire population of countries has not collapsed into one narrow band. Keeping the same 159 countries means this change cannot be attributed to richer-data countries entering later years. Density peaks depend on smoothing, so the robust conclusions concern the broad shift and persistent spread, not the exact number of bumps.",
"The median country's adoption rises by 69.17 percentage points from 2000 to 2020, but the broad final distribution still contains unequal outcomes. A higher center is not the same as convergence."),
10: (
"Faceting asks whether the pooled shift reflects comparable movement inside every region. Inspect the horizontal locations and spreads of ridges at matched years, rather than comparing peak heights as counts. The small Oceania and South America groups contribute only eight and ten countries, making their smooth curves particularly sensitive to individual observations. A region with a compact high-adoption distribution and one with a broad lower distribution can be concealed by a single global ridge. These panels explain composition, not independent statistical clusters.",
"Regional distributions show which groups contribute to the global adoption shift and which retain broad variation. Apparent peaks in the smallest regions are weak evidence unless supported by raw values and smoothing checks."),
11: (
"The atlas places all eight animation dates on one common scale, making it useful as a static navigation aid to the GIFs. It does not add observations between dates. A feature visible in two panels may have weakened, disappeared or returned during the 13-day gap; persistence cannot be inferred from recurrence in snapshots. The 92-day diagrams and run-length analysis answer that temporal question directly. This atlas therefore remains useful supporting context, but has lower incremental value once those complete-daily analyses and the animations are supplied.",
"The atlas supports consistent comparison of eight sampled fields, but not event duration. Its main role is a static companion to the GIFs; full-daily analyses provide the additional temporal evidence."),
12: (
"With 68.7% of valid cells dry, the dominant rainfall distinction is zero versus positive precipitation before any subtle positive-value contrast. Logarithmic mapping exposes differences among smaller positive amounts, while a linear scale emphasizes the larger values. The 1.01% of cells above the displayed rain limit cannot be ranked visually after saturation. The temperature comparison changes both palette and classing, so it demonstrates their combined effect rather than isolating discretization as a single cause. This limitation should accompany any claim about which rendering is clearer.",
"Rainfall normalization changes which positive amounts are distinguishable, while most cells remain dry. Preserve zero and saturation information; the temperature pair shows a combined palette/classing change rather than an isolated discretization experiment."),
13: (
"The local scale stretches each day's range across the palette, making within-day variation more visible even on a relatively uniform day. That same benefit can make a cooler day look as visually warm as a hotter one. The narrowest and widest ranges quoted in the caption are descriptive of the sampled dates, not climate-normal variability. Shared extrema are preferable for comparing absolute temperatures across dates; local extrema answer a different question about spatial contrast within each date. Neither choice changes the observations.",
"Use the shared scale to compare temperatures between dates and the local scale to inspect contrast within a date. Similar colors in locally normalized maps are not evidence of similar absolute temperatures."),
14: (
"The 5-degree contours offer more threshold crossings to follow than the 10-degree contours, but also introduce more lines and labels that can obscure one another. Filled bands change the task from tracing an exact boundary to comparing areas inside temperature intervals. Compare the same location in all four panels to attribute differences to interval or fill. The contours interpolate crossings between sampled cells; a smooth line is not evidence that the original field was measured at that finer resolution. This controlled comparison is required methodological evidence.",
"Contour interval and filling determine whether boundaries or broad regions are easier to inspect. Finer contours add display detail, not new measurements, and should be chosen for the question rather than visual complexity."),
15: (
"The three columns thin an already subsampled field, so 1-, 2- and 3-degree arrow spacing should not be confused with native weather resolution. Sparse arrows reduce overlap but can remove local directional structure. Equal-length arrows recover the visibility of weak-wind directions while deliberately discarding length as a speed channel; color then carries that information. Because the same date, extent and speed scale are used, these differences can be attributed to drawing choices rather than different weather conditions.",
"Thinning trades directional detail for legibility, while equal lengths make weak-wind directions visible at the cost of a length-based speed cue. Read magnitude from the key and color convention, not arrow prominence alone."),
16: (
"Using all daily fields gives a mean September-minus-July Tmax difference of -4.64 C and cooling over 99.66% of the weighted footprint. The former two-date view disagrees in sign over 18.26% of area, which demonstrates the sensitivity of an apparent change to temporal selection. Sector ECDFs then show that cooling is not spatially uniform even where its sign is consistent. This figure does not reject daily snapshots; it limits what a snapshot comparison can answer when the stated question concerns complete months.",
"Complete-month means show cooling across 99.66% of sampled area, yet an endpoint-only map disagrees in sign over 18.26%. Temporal aggregation changes the supported conclusion; this within-2023 difference is not a climate anomaly."),
17: (
"The western, central and eastern sectors summarize different collections of cells, so the ribbons describe spatial spread inside each sector. A wide ribbon means that locations differ on that day; it does not mean the mean was estimated with poor statistical confidence. The central sector's endpoint change is only -0.15 C, but the path between those endpoints still contains substantial movement. This is why the full trajectory contributes information beyond the monthly difference: a small net change can conceal large intervening fluctuations.",
"A near-zero central-sector endpoint change does not imply stable daily temperatures. The 92-day paths expose intervening fluctuations, while the ribbons describe differences between locations rather than uncertainty in an estimated mean."),
18: (
"The area-weighted monthly means decrease from 31.19 to 30.16 to 26.55 C, but each panel preserves where warmer and cooler regions occur. Fixed 25 and 35 C isolines provide recognizable thresholds on top of the shared color scale. The result complements, rather than duplicates, the difference map: these panels show absolute monthly levels, whereas the difference map shows the amount of change. The mean of daily maxima must not be paraphrased as the mean temperature across all hours of a month.",
"Monthly mean daily maxima decline from 31.19 C in July to 26.55 C in September, with spatial structure still visible. Absolute-level maps locate the remaining warmer regions that a change-only map cannot identify."),
19: (
"July and August have similar area-mean accumulation, 75.26 and 75.82 mm, while September is lower at 57.08 mm. A similar domain mean does not imply the rain fell in the same places, which is why the maps are retained. September also has one fewer day: these are actual monthly totals, so a claim specifically about daily intensity should instead compare day-normalized means. Masking incomplete months prevents missing observations from being silently interpreted as dry days. The eight animation snapshots cannot reproduce these sums.",
"July and August have similar area-mean rainfall totals, while September is lower; the maps reveal their different spatial allocation. Interpret these as complete-month accumulation, with 31/31/30-day durations explicitly retained."),
20: (
"Threshold frequency answers how often a location experiences a daily maximum of at least 35 C, rather than how warm its average day is. The area-day fraction falls from 22.8% in July and 22.2% in August to 8.9% in September. Two cells can have a similar monthly mean but different numbers of threshold exceedances. This figure remains a useful temperature-only baseline for the later compound analysis: adding a low-rainfall condition narrows the question and cannot increase the joint frequency above the hot-day frequency on identical support.",
"The >=35 C area-day fraction is much lower in September, but this measures frequency alone. It supplies the temperature-only baseline needed to interpret the stricter hot-and-dry condition and its persistence."),
21: (
"Reading across a longitude follows its entire 92-day band-average trajectory, whereas reading vertically compares locations at the same date. The paired rain panel exposes temporal bursts that monthly totals combine. The largest rain band mean, 95.20 mm on September 16 near -67.27 degrees, belongs to a particular averaging column, not to an identified storm or the full country. Columns near the footprint edge may contain fewer cells; their support counts are saved for inspection. Latitude averaging deliberately sacrifices local detail to reveal time-space organization.",
"The paired diagrams preserve when and where daily temperature and rainfall patterns occur, which monthly maps suppress. Their values are averages over a fixed latitude band, so they cannot identify local extremes or track individual storms."),
22: (
"The three positive-rain curves are visually similar, so the chart's clearest additional information comes from the omitted zero mass: dry grid-days rise from 68.8% in July to 72.3% in September, a 3.5-percentage-point increase. That difference helps explain why similar conditional positive distributions can coexist with different monthly accumulation. However, equal grid-day weighting differs from the approximate area weighting used elsewhere. The curve comparison is descriptive, and its modest visual separation makes it a reasonable candidate for a compact methods note rather than a full main-result figure.",
"Similar positive-rain distributions can coexist with different dryness: September has 72.3% zero-rain grid-days versus July's 68.8%. The conditioning matters more here than small differences between the nearly overlapping curves."),
23: (
"The rose aggregates direction and speed together, which the scalar temperature or rainfall maps cannot supply. A tall sector means a larger weighted share of noncalm grid-day summaries from that direction, and its stacked colors separate speed classes. The radial height represents frequency; the visually expanding wedge area should not be read as proportional frequency. Pooling the entire western box suppresses geographic differences, so the rose is a distributional complement to the quiver maps, not a replacement for their spatial structure.",
"Wind roses summarize the joint distribution of source direction and speed, whereas quiver maps retain where those winds occur. Read sector height as frequency and remember that from-direction is opposite to arrow flow-to direction."),
24: (
"Because every undirected edge appears twice, 590 edges produce 1,180 dark off-diagonal matrix entries. Of the actual edges, 249 are within regions and 341 cross regions; therefore supplied geography does not partition the similarity network into isolated regional groups. That raw split is not a measure of preferential within-region linking because region sizes determine how many possible pairs exist. The matrix is valuable because it exposes exact adjacency without layout-induced proximity or edge crossings; its ordered blocks are a viewing convention, not discovered communities.",
"Most stored similarity edges cross supplied region boundaries (341 of 590). The matrix verifies connections independently of layout, but raw within/between counts require possible-pair normalization before interpreting regional affinity."),
25: (
"The dumbbell offers an efficient regional summary that the country-change scatter cannot provide at a glance. Asia's median shifts by 26.55 percentage points, the largest increase among classified regions on this matched panel. A movement in the regional median does not mean each country moved by that amount or even moved upward. The 175-country panel also differs from the 159-country long-run panel, so small differences from the horizon medians need not indicate an error. Each plot answers a different coverage and time-span question.",
"Asia has the largest classified-region median shift on the 175-country matched panel, at 26.55 percentage points. Retain this concise group summary alongside country-level changes; it does not describe every country's trajectory."),
26: (
"The figure places broadband and adoption on the most precise position channels, then asks whether mobile intensity and landline scale distinguish otherwise similar profiles. The same 175 countries yield adoption-broadband rho 0.831 and adoption-mobile rho 0.531. This supports the choice to inspect fixed and mobile indicators separately, rather than claiming that a high mobile count alone explains connectivity. Regional facets improve visibility, but the quoted correlations are pooled over all matched countries; they should not be presented as coefficients independently established in every region.",
"On the matched panel, adoption is more strongly associated with broadband levels (rho 0.831) than mobile intensity (0.531). Four measurements expose different infrastructure profiles at similar adoption, without identifying the cause of those differences."),
27: (
"Exactly 126 countries lie in the adoption-gain, landline-decline quadrant. That 72% share is a transparent descriptive count, not a fitted model. The broadband-adoption association falls from rho 0.831 for 2020 levels to 0.054 for 2015-2020 changes on identical countries. Stable cross-country differences, different starting levels and the timing of changes can make these comparisons diverge, but the chart cannot identify which explanation is responsible. It therefore qualifies the level-based story without establishing that broadband growth has no effect.",
"Adoption rises while landline intensity falls in 72% of matched countries, yet broadband and adoption changes are only weakly rank-associated. Strong differences between countries must not be read as a causal account of change within countries."),
28: (
"The matrix checks the four-variable scatter's less precise color and area channels using pairwise positions. Landlines and broadband have rho 0.866, while adoption and broadband have rho 0.831; points still vary around both relationships. Mobile shows weaker pooled associations with the fixed indicators. The diagonal histograms reveal skewed distributions that can give a few countries substantial visual influence. Rank correlation reduces dependence on the exact numerical spacing, but it remains a descriptive monotonic summary and does not replace inspecting the scatter for exceptions or regional composition.",
"Fixed infrastructure measures share strong pooled rank associations, but the scatter cells preserve their exceptions and distributional skew. The matrix validates relationships that bubble color and size alone are harder to assess precisely."),
29: (
"July 27 and September 30 were selected by a reproducible criterion: the highest and lowest sampled-area fractions meeting the hot/dry definition. Their 34.56% versus 1.75% values quantify why the dates contrast; the maps then show where temperature, rain contours and vector fields coexist. This does not establish that wind produced the difference. Tmax is a daily maximum, rain is a daily accumulation, and wind is another daily product, so temporal alignment by date must not be confused with simultaneous observations of a weather event.",
"The objectively selected dates contrast 34.56% versus 1.75% hot/dry area, while four separate channels expose their spatial context. Co-located daily temperature, rain and wind summaries support comparison, not event attribution or causal explanation."),
30: (
"A frequency percentage counts qualifying days but ignores their order. The run-length map restores that information and finds a maximum continuous run of 67 days within the observation window. That maximum applies to at least one sampled cell, not the entire footprint; the mean joint area-day fraction is only 16.27%. The sensitivity panel shows how frequency decreases under stricter temperature thresholds and increases under a more permissive rain cutoff. Thus the detailed numerical result depends on the declared definition even though the distinction between frequency and persistence does not.",
"Hot/dry conditions occupy 16.27% of sampled area-days, yet a location can sustain a 67-day run. Frequency alone hides concentration in time; retain threshold sensitivity and distinguish a local maximum from a domain-wide experience."),
31: (
"Kenya's increase from 0.32% to 24.72% is substantial but never reaches the 50% reference in this panel. Subtraction therefore remains negative throughout, and folding retains a red sign encoding even while the original line rises. The intermediate panel is what makes the transformation auditable: readers can match a year before and after folding. This is useful explanatory information about the visualization, not an additional discovery about adoption beyond the values already shown. It can become a compact methods inset if main-report space is limited.",
"Kenya's adoption rises while its horizon remains red because every value is below 50%. The construction figure prevents a specific misreading of the required horizons; its contribution is methodological rather than a separate data finding."),
32: (
"All three KDE curves use the same 159 observations, so their different small peaks must come from bandwidth choice rather than changing country data. The empirical distribution and its median of 71.37% provide an unsmoothed reference. Features that vanish at a nearby bandwidth should not be described as established adoption groups. This check improves the credibility of the ridgeline interpretation, but its conclusion can be reported succinctly in methods if a full sensitivity figure would crowd out a stronger substantive result.",
"Bandwidth changes the apparent density peaks while the raw median remains 71.37%. Base the ridgeline conclusion on the broad distributional shift and empirical values, not on minor modes produced by one smoothing choice."),
}

assert set(DETAILS) == set(range(1,33))

